Vulnerability Title

Improper Email Domain Validation Allows Unauthorized Domains in Coverage Details

Vulnerability Category
Input Validation Vulnerability
Business Logic Validation Failure
Improper Access Restriction
OWASP Top 10 Category (2021/2025 Mapping)
A04:2021 – Insecure Design
A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration
CVSS v3.1 Score
6.5 – Medium
CVSS Vector
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N
Description

The application is intended to restrict email addresses entered in the Coverage Details email field to only authorized corporate domains such as:

@morganstanley.com

However, during testing it was identified that the application accepts email addresses from arbitrary external domains instead of enforcing the intended domain restriction.

For example, the application accepts email addresses such as:

test@gmail.com
user@yahoo.com
attacker@evil.com

This indicates insufficient server-side validation of allowed email domains.

Steps to Reproduce
Navigate to the Coverage Details section.
Locate the email address input field.

Enter an unauthorized email address such as:

attacker@gmail.com
Submit/save the form.

Observe that the application accepts the external domain email successfully instead of restricting it to:

@morganstanley.com
Impact

Successful exploitation may result in:

Unauthorized external users being added to workflows
Leakage of sensitive business communications
Unauthorized notification delivery
Exposure of confidential transaction or operational data
Violation of internal security policies and compliance requirements
Increased phishing/social engineering risk through external recipients

If this field is used for notifications, approvals, confirmations, or document delivery, sensitive corporate information may be sent outside the organization.

Mitigation

Implement strict server-side validation to ensure only approved corporate domains are accepted.

Recommended Controls

Validate email addresses against an allowlist:

@morganstanley.com
Reject all unauthorized external domains.
Perform validation on both client-side and server-side.
Normalize email input before validation:
Trim spaces
Convert to lowercase

Use regex/domain validation such as:

^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@morganstanley\.com$
Log attempts to use unauthorized domains.
Implement centralized validation for all email fields across the application.
Example Validation Logic
if (!email.toLowerCase().endsWith("@morganstanley.com")) {
    throw new ValidationException("Only Morgan Stanley email addresses are allowed");
}
